    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d493">

                <pb facs="0719" n="699" sameAs="frus:frus1950v07/pg699"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.5622/9–650: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">niact</hi>
                        
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-09-06T16:00:00+03:00">September 6,
                            1950—4 p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 6—11:18 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>622. Eyes only for Secretary. At his request I called on Vishinsky at 1 p. m.
                    (Moscow time) today. He opened by referring to statement made by US
                    Representative Security Council September 5 on Soviet plane incident off Korea
                    September 4,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d493-f1"/> describing it as incorrect.</p>
                <p>He then read me via interpreter Soviet note to US Government, in substance
                    alleging Soviet plane unarmed training mission 140 kilometers distant Korean
                    coastline. Presentation followed in reverse our note April 18 Baltic
                        plane,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d493-f2"/> demanded investigation
                    indemnification punishment those responsible.</p>
                <p>I replied as follows:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“The information at my disposal indicates that the question to which you
                        refer pertains to defensive action by United Nations Forces operating in the
                        Korean area in accordance with resolutions of the United Nations Security
                        Council and that it has, in fact, been brought to the attention of the
                        Security Council for appropriate consideration. I am therefore not in a
                        position to accept on behalf of the United States Government the
                        communication of the Soviet Government on this subject. It would appear to
                        be appropriate for any representations on this question to be presented to
                        the United Nations Security Council.”</p>
                </quote>
                <pb facs="0720" n="700" xml:id="pg700"/>
                <p>During further half hour Vishinsky attempted persuade me accept note using
                    various arguments. I consistently adhered position subject matter under UN
                    competence in Security Council where Soviets should make their representations.
                    Details later.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
